<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2013/05/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>runtime.liblouis</id>
    <version>$version$</version>
    <title>runtime.liblouis</title>
    <authors>Liblouis contributors.</authors>
    <owners>Nota</owners>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <license type="file">LICENSE</license>
    <projectUrl>https://liblouis.io/</projectUrl>
    <repository type="git" url="https://github.com/notalib/LibLouis.NET" />
    <copyright>Liblouis 2010 - 2025</copyright>
    <tags>liblouis braille native</tags>
    <description>Note: Do not reference this package directly, it is pulled in by LibLouis.NET.

Metapackage that brings in the native liblouis binary for every supported runtime identifier. The .NET host selects the binary matching the runtime it is running on; publishing with an explicit runtime identifier narrows this down to a single binary.

The Liblouis software suite provides an open-source braille translator, back-translator and formatter for a large number of languages and braille codes. It is a set of libraries designed for use in any of a number of applications, both free and commercial. It is written in C so that it does not require a runtime environment and hence can be used in applications written in high-level languages such as Java and Python. Packaged by Nota.</description>

    <!--
      Hand-written rather than generated from PackageReference items on purpose.

      A metapackage is pure metadata, and declaring the dependencies here means packing it needs no
      restore. That matters because no single CI job ever has all seven runtime packages available:
      the Linux job builds the Linux and Windows RIDs, the macOS job builds the macOS RIDs. With a
      nuspec the metapackage can be produced by any job, in any order, without a local feed.

      Versions are pinned exactly so a consumer can never end up with a native binary from a
      different liblouis release than the metapackage advertises.
    -->
    <dependencies>
      <group targetFramework=".NETStandard2.0">
        <dependency id="runtime.linux-x86.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.linux-x64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.linux-arm64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.win-x86.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.win-x64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.win-arm64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.osx-x64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
        <dependency id="runtime.osx-arm64.liblouis" version="[$version$]" />
      </group>
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>

  <files>
    <file src="LICENSE" target="LICENSE" />
    <file src="../build/_._" target="lib/netstandard2.0/_._" />
  </files>
</package>
